 "admissions_overview": {
  "summary": "Admission to South African public universities is handled by each university directly; there is no single central application service. Applicants usually apply online to each university, and are considered mainly on their National Senior Certificate (NSC) results, with a Bachelor's pass needed for degree study. Most degrees also set minimum levels in specific subjects (for example Mathematics for engineering, science and commerce, and Physical Sciences or Life Sciences for health and science programmes), and some programmes add the National Benchmark Tests (NBTs), an interview or a portfolio. Applicants with Cambridge, IB or other school-leaving qualifications are assessed separately. Entry requirements are published in each university's prospectus, so check the current prospectus. NSFAS (the National Student Financial Aid Scheme) is the main state bursary and loan scheme for eligible students at public universities.",
  "official_url": "https://www.nsfas.org.za",
  "notes": "Application opening and closing dates, programme lists and the minimum requirements for each programme change every year, and many programmes close early. Always check the current prospectus and admissions page of each university. Apply for NSFAS funding separately through the NSFAS website."
 },
